1
00:00:00,500 --> 00:00:05,460
Fifteen million foreigners visited
Korea in just eight months. A record.

2
00:00:05,810 --> 00:00:08,530
And they didn't just visit.
They caught something.

3
00:00:08,880 --> 00:00:10,880
It's called "Seoul fever."

4
00:00:11,230 --> 00:00:16,270
Hello, world. I'm Master K, and
this is the Four Eyes Report.

5
00:00:16,620 --> 00:00:20,907
Coming up: a fever, a chip
boom, a holiday for an alphabet,

6
00:00:20,907 --> 00:00:24,540
a very fast country, and
why Koreans ask your age.

7
00:00:24,890 --> 00:00:30,077
Let's start with the fever. Our current
affairs correspondent, Ms. Nonfic.

8
00:00:30,077 --> 00:00:32,810
She reads the corrections page for fun.

9
00:00:33,160 --> 00:00:35,896
Good evening. From January to August,

10
00:00:35,896 --> 00:00:41,146
fifteen point oh five million foreign
visitors came to Korea. A record.

11
00:00:41,146 --> 00:00:44,548
That is more people than
live in Pennsylvania.

12
00:00:44,548 --> 00:00:46,840
Pennsylvania has not commented.

13
00:00:47,190 --> 00:00:49,190
Somebody call Pennsylvania!

14
00:00:49,540 --> 00:00:53,522
August alone: two point two
five million. Another record.

15
00:00:53,522 --> 00:00:56,597
Much of the growth came
from Southeast Asia,

16
00:00:56,597 --> 00:01:00,020
thanks to easier entry
rules and higher spending.

17
00:01:00,370 --> 00:01:04,370
Hello, Singapore, Malaysia, and
the Philippines! We see you!

18
00:01:04,720 --> 00:01:10,400
And a new word came with them. "Seoul
fever," and its cousin, "Busan fever."

19
00:01:10,750 --> 00:01:15,306
Both words describe travelers who
miss the city after they go home.

20
00:01:15,306 --> 00:01:17,550
In other words: repeat customers.

21
00:01:17,900 --> 00:01:21,239
I'm a doctor. Not that kind.
But I have a diagnosis:

22
00:01:21,239 --> 00:01:22,780
it's good for the Seoul.

23
00:01:23,130 --> 00:01:24,010
Please don't.

24
00:01:24,360 --> 00:01:28,840
Seoul-searching. Seoul food.
Seoul mate. I have a whole list.

25
00:01:29,190 --> 00:01:32,909
For the record, I heard
all of that. Now, rankings.

26
00:01:32,909 --> 00:01:39,910
Agoda's booking data for the first half of the
year puts Seoul fourth in Asia for repeat visits.

27
00:01:40,260 --> 00:01:41,700
Fourth! Who beat us?

28
00:01:42,050 --> 00:01:46,370
Tokyo, Bangkok, and Bali. Osaka is fifth.

29
00:01:46,720 --> 00:01:50,000
Fourth place. The saddest
number at the Olympics.

30
00:01:50,350 --> 00:01:55,750
Analysts credit the range: K-content,
festivals, food, beauty, shopping,

31
00:01:55,750 --> 00:01:58,750
and convenience stores that never close.

32
00:01:59,100 --> 00:02:04,060
So the fever has a cause: a lot
going on, and a snack at every hour.

33
00:02:04,410 --> 00:02:08,490
Symptoms include booking your second
trip before the first one ends.

34
00:02:08,840 --> 00:02:10,680
Treatment: none known.

35
00:02:12,130 --> 00:02:18,152
If you catch it, where do you go?
Our travel expert: Professor Indoor.

36
00:02:18,152 --> 00:02:20,130
Self-certified. Online.

37
00:02:20,480 --> 00:02:21,360
I've been there!

38
00:02:21,710 --> 00:02:22,670
Physically?

39
00:02:23,020 --> 00:02:23,980
...Emotionally.

40
00:02:24,330 --> 00:02:25,177
Okay, facts.

41
00:02:25,177 --> 00:02:30,401
Seoul is running a welcome campaign
for tourists through October eleventh,

42
00:02:30,401 --> 00:02:35,977
with a help desk near Myeongdong Station
that offers interpretation in English,

43
00:02:35,977 --> 00:02:37,530
Chinese, and Japanese.

44
00:02:37,880 --> 00:02:41,000
Help desks and interpreters.
That's service.

45
00:02:41,350 --> 00:02:44,033
From October seventh to eleventh,

46
00:02:44,033 --> 00:02:51,270
the K-Royal Culture Festival turns the five
grand palaces and Jongmyo Shrine into stages.

47
00:02:51,620 --> 00:02:55,323
There is also a moonlight
tour of Changdeokgung Palace,

48
00:02:55,323 --> 00:02:58,420
with sessions in English
for foreign visitors.

49
00:02:58,770 --> 00:03:01,810
A palace at night. That's so romantic.

50
00:03:02,160 --> 00:03:08,092
I watched the whole thing on a live
stream. Best seat in the house. No line,

51
00:03:08,092 --> 00:03:11,760
no crowds, and the snacks
are twelve feet away.

52
00:03:12,110 --> 00:03:14,990
That is... one way to travel.

53
00:03:15,340 --> 00:03:18,379
Now, the souvenir of the season: a cap.

54
00:03:18,379 --> 00:03:23,522
Seoul's city brand teamed up with
a traditional-knot design brand,

55
00:03:23,522 --> 00:03:29,835
and one post about it got two point seven
million views on Japanese social media.

56
00:03:29,835 --> 00:03:30,380
No ads.

57
00:03:30,730 --> 00:03:32,810
Let me guess. It's Seoul-d out.

58
00:03:33,160 --> 00:03:35,320
I wouldn't know. I've never been there.

59
00:03:35,670 --> 00:03:36,790
Emotionally?

60
00:03:37,140 --> 00:03:39,300
Emotionally, I own three.

61
00:03:40,750 --> 00:03:45,790
Now, another record. Max Rise,
our money and business analyst.

62
00:03:46,140 --> 00:03:48,060
Good news. The line went up.

63
00:03:48,410 --> 00:03:53,690
September exports: a hundred
twenty-one billion dollars. A record.

64
00:03:53,690 --> 00:03:55,610
Up eighty-three percent.

65
00:03:55,960 --> 00:03:57,560
That's almost double!

66
00:03:57,910 --> 00:04:02,197
Chips did the heavy lifting.
Over sixty billion dollars.

67
00:04:02,197 --> 00:04:05,030
Almost half of everything Korea sold.

68
00:04:05,380 --> 00:04:08,900
Finally, a chip Americans
and Koreans both love.

69
00:04:09,250 --> 00:04:10,530
Different chips.

70
00:04:10,880 --> 00:04:12,000
Mine come with dip.

71
00:04:12,350 --> 00:04:16,662
AI is hungry. Korea makes
the memory chips it eats.

72
00:04:16,662 --> 00:04:19,790
And Korean chips remember everything.

73
00:04:20,140 --> 00:04:21,980
Unlike me at the grocery store.

74
00:04:22,330 --> 00:04:26,628
Nine months in, Korea has
already beaten all of last year.

75
00:04:26,628 --> 00:04:29,370
A trillion dollars is next. Limit up!

76
00:04:29,720 --> 00:04:32,520
A trillion! How many zeros is that?

77
00:04:32,870 --> 00:04:35,110
Twelve. Thank you for the math.

78
00:04:35,460 --> 00:04:38,660
Seoul fever. Chip fever. Limit up!

79
00:04:39,010 --> 00:04:40,130
Is there a cure?

80
00:04:40,480 --> 00:04:41,760
Not on my chart.

81
00:04:43,210 --> 00:04:48,474
Next, a holiday you might not
know: Hangul Day, October ninth.

82
00:04:48,474 --> 00:04:51,530
To explain it, our historian, Dr. H.

83
00:04:51,880 --> 00:04:54,520
Dr. H, what does the H stand for?

84
00:04:54,870 --> 00:04:56,070
...That's classified.

85
00:04:56,420 --> 00:04:58,940
Actually, Hangul Day is a public holiday.

86
00:04:58,940 --> 00:05:02,260
Korea takes a day off to
celebrate its writing system.

87
00:05:02,610 --> 00:05:06,610
A holiday for an alphabet? In
America, we just get a song.

88
00:05:06,960 --> 00:05:09,920
The ABC song is not a holiday.

89
00:05:10,270 --> 00:05:12,750
It should be. It has a really good tune.

90
00:05:13,100 --> 00:05:17,587
Before the fourteen-forties, writing
in Korea meant Chinese characters,

91
00:05:17,587 --> 00:05:19,420
and learning them took years.

92
00:05:19,770 --> 00:05:26,010
King Sejong the Great created Hangul in the fourteen-forties
so that ordinary people could read and write.

93
00:05:26,360 --> 00:05:27,881
And here is the secret.

94
00:05:27,881 --> 00:05:32,840
The consonants copy the shape your
mouth and tongue make when you say them.

95
00:05:33,190 --> 00:05:34,696
The letters are diagrams?

96
00:05:34,696 --> 00:05:37,830
So it's basically the first
user-friendly interface.

97
00:05:38,180 --> 00:05:42,980
Version one. Still no patch notes
after five hundred eighty years.

98
00:05:43,330 --> 00:05:48,210
Today it has twenty-four letters:
fourteen consonants and ten vowels.

99
00:05:48,560 --> 00:05:51,200
Learners say you can
learn to read it in a day.

100
00:05:51,550 --> 00:05:54,270
Let me test that. Write your name.

101
00:05:54,620 --> 00:05:55,260
Done!

102
00:05:55,610 --> 00:05:58,330
It says "Kang-peu-ri." Close enough.

103
00:05:59,780 --> 00:06:01,434
Time for a new segment:

104
00:06:01,434 --> 00:06:06,180
"Is This Normal in Korea?" Our
expert witness is back: Ms. Nonfic.

105
00:06:06,530 --> 00:06:08,287
Exhibit A: the sidewalk.

106
00:06:08,287 --> 00:06:13,410
Everyone in Seoul walks like they're
late for a flight. They might be.

107
00:06:13,760 --> 00:06:16,720
That's "ppalli-ppalli."
It means "hurry, hurry."

108
00:06:17,070 --> 00:06:19,774
It's less a habit than
an operating system.

109
00:06:19,774 --> 00:06:22,164
Delivery apps promise food in minutes.

110
00:06:22,164 --> 00:06:27,070
Some people press the elevator's close
button before the doors finish opening.

111
00:06:27,420 --> 00:06:29,740
They're not impatient. They're optimistic.

112
00:06:30,090 --> 00:06:35,610
In America, you wait for the pizza.
In Korea, the pizza waits for you.

113
00:06:35,960 --> 00:06:38,040
So, is this normal in Korea?

114
00:06:38,390 --> 00:06:41,430
Verdict: normal. Please keep moving.

115
00:06:41,780 --> 00:06:43,460
Welcome to Korea.

116
00:06:44,910 --> 00:06:48,910
Last topic. A question every
foreigner hears within five minutes.

117
00:06:49,260 --> 00:06:50,540
"How old are you?"

118
00:06:50,890 --> 00:06:52,890
In Korea, that's grammar.

119
00:06:53,240 --> 00:06:56,739
Korean has speech levels.
To pick the right one,

120
00:06:56,739 --> 00:07:02,280
you need to know who is older. So the
question is not nosy. It is a setting.

121
00:07:02,630 --> 00:07:07,733
Until 2023, Korea also had "Korean
age": you are one at birth,

122
00:07:07,733 --> 00:07:11,931
and everyone gets a year
older every January first.

123
00:07:11,931 --> 00:07:16,870
A baby born on December
thirty-first turns two the next day.

124
00:07:17,220 --> 00:07:19,220
Two days old and already two!

125
00:07:19,570 --> 00:07:25,291
In June 2023, Korea switched official
documents to international age.

126
00:07:25,291 --> 00:07:28,690
Everyone became one or two years younger.

127
00:07:29,040 --> 00:07:34,080
Korea invented the cheapest anti-aging
treatment in history: legislation.

128
00:07:34,430 --> 00:07:37,870
Social life did not
change. People still ask.

129
00:07:38,220 --> 00:07:44,401
One more thing. Koreans say "our."
Our mom, our house, our school.

130
00:07:44,401 --> 00:07:45,900
Even "our wife."

131
00:07:46,250 --> 00:07:52,090
"Our" signals belonging, not
ownership. It means: we share this.

132
00:07:52,440 --> 00:07:55,800
So he's not sharing his
wife. He's sharing the word.

133
00:07:56,150 --> 00:07:57,750
Please don't say that in English.

134
00:07:59,200 --> 00:08:03,883
So here's my take. Fifteen million
visitors, a record chip boom,

135
00:08:03,883 --> 00:08:08,712
an alphabet with its own holiday.
Different stories, same pattern:

136
00:08:08,712 --> 00:08:11,200
the world is showing up for Korea.

137
00:08:11,550 --> 00:08:17,373
And Korea's answer is very Korean:
"Welcome. Our home is your home.

138
00:08:17,373 --> 00:08:18,590
Please hurry."

139
00:08:18,940 --> 00:08:22,940
Tell us in the comments: what's
the first thing you'd ask a Korean?

140
00:08:23,290 --> 00:08:27,363
Subscribe, and we'll see
you Wednesday. Stay curious.

141
00:08:27,363 --> 00:08:29,130
Keep your lenses clean.
